    {
      "id": "library/10665-369381/blocks/prose-sec-001",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Guidance overview",
      "pageStart": 9,
      "pageEnd": 11,
      "target": "../sections/sec-001-guidance-overview.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "WHO TOOLKIT FOR ANALYSIS AND USE OF ROUTINE HEALTH FACILITY DATA \nThis document is part of the WHO Toolkit for analysis and use of routine health facility data – a set of capacity-\nbuilding resources to optimize the analysis and use of data collected from health facilities through routine health \ninformation systems (RHIS). The Toolkit is a collaborative effort by multiple WHO technical programmes and \npartners. It promotes an integrated, standards-based approach to facility data analysis, using a limited set of \nstandardized core indicators with recommended analyses, visualizations and dashbo",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Places the document within WHO's Toolkit for analysis and use of routine health facility data, which promotes standardized core indicators, analyses and dashboards across programmes. It describes the toolkit's components and explains that this guidance offers district and facility managers examples of using routine data for decisions, without covering district management in full.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-369381/blocks/prose-sec-002",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Learning objectives",
      "pageStart": 11,
      "pageEnd": 13,
      "target": "../sections/sec-002-learning-objectives.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "This guidance illustrates integrated analysis of RHIS data at district and facility levels. It aims to build understanding \nand skills in a recommended analytic approach that: \n\ty\t\nfocuses on a minimum set of indicators organized into three cross-cutting groups; \n\ty\t\n\u0007uses standard visualizations to facilitate data interpretation: charts and tables organized into integrated \ndashboards that can be automatically updated in an electronic database;  \n\ty\t\n\u0007supports use of dashboards for decision-making as part of district meetings, supervision visits and feedback \nto health facilities.  \nThe guida",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "States the learning objectives of the guidance: a focused set of indicators in three groups, standard visualizations in integrated dashboards and their use in meetings and supervision. It defines the target audience of district managers and analysts and lists suggested reading.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-369381/blocks/prose-sec-003",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "District health system",
      "pageStart": 13,
      "pageEnd": 13,
      "target": "../sections/sec-003-11-district-health-system.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Districts are administrative units. They come in all shapes and sizes, from a few thousand people to multi-million \npopulations. In many countries, districts are the main subnational administrative unit and the level that manages \nthe public health service delivery system.\nAccording to the WHO, the district health system [5] is “a network of primary care health facilities that deliver a \ncomprehensive range of promotive, preventive and curative health care services to a defined population with active \nparticipation of the community and under the supervision of a district hospital1 and district",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes the district health system as a network of primary care facilities under a district hospital and management team, possibly including private providers, and explains how the term is used in the guidance.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-369381/blocks/prose-sec-004",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Data needs of district and facility managers",
      "pageStart": 13,
      "pageEnd": 14,
      "target": "../sections/sec-004-12-data-needs-of-district-and-facility-managers.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "District and facility managers are responsible for ensuring that the health services under their management are \nappropriately delivered. This may include ensuring that:  \n\ty\t\ndistrict health services are in line with national and subnational policies, priorities and standards;\n\ty\t\ndistrict health services quickly detect and respond to unusual events and changing needs; \n\ty\t\n\u0007all segments of the district population have access to the health services they need, at the required standards \nof quality;\n\ty\t\ndistrict health services achieve required targets; and \n\ty\t\nthe resources needed to provide",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Lists the responsibilities of district and facility managers, such as meeting policies, responding to events, ensuring access and using resources efficiently, and their need for routine data and context to fulfil them.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-369381/blocks/prose-sec-005",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Data sources at subnational level",
      "pageStart": 14,
      "pageEnd": 14,
      "target": "../sections/sec-005-13-data-sources-at-subnational-level.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "This guidance focuses on aggregate data reported through the RHIS. However, at district and facility levels, health \ninformation may be available from various sources as part of the broader country health information system \n(HIS). The HIS brings together data from multiple sources, including the RHIS, health facility assessments, client \nexperience surveys, household surveys, censuses, civil registration systems, surveillance systems, and other \nadministrative data sources [6]1. \nVarious data sources are mentioned briefly in the following section; some are needed for calculation of some of \nt",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains that the guidance focuses on aggregate routine health information system data while noting other sources within the broader health information system, such as surveys, censuses and surveillance.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-369381/blocks/prose-sec-006",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Routine health information system (RHIS)\u0004",
      "pageStart": 14,
      "pageEnd": 15,
      "target": "../sections/sec-006-131-routine-health-information-system-rhis.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Health facilities routinely collect data on the diseases and other health conditions for which people seek care. \nThey also collect routine data on facility activities (outputs such as number of outpatient department visits, \nnumber of vaccine doses given) and the outcomes of those activities (e.g. number of tuberculosis (TB) patients \ncured, number of inpatient deaths). These data are aggregated and reported at regular intervals through the \nRHIS to higher levels of the health system. Data should be analysed and used at all levels. While RHIS data are \ncommonly reported each month, the freque",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes the routine data that health facilities collect and report on diseases, service outputs and outcomes, and how often they are reported. It also outlines related information systems, including surveillance, human resources, logistics and financial management systems.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-369381/blocks/prose-sec-007",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Population data",
      "pageStart": 15,
      "pageEnd": 16,
      "target": "../sections/sec-007-134-population-data.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Population data serve as denominators for many RHIS indicators, e.g. utilization rates, coverage. It is important \nthat all managers and analysts have an estimate of the population that the district system is expected to cover. \nHowever, there are often challenges in obtaining reliable population data. Population estimates based on \nprojections of census data may be out-of-date or inaccurate; in general, the smaller the geographic area, the less \nreliable the population data. Especially challenging is the estimation of the population living in the “catchment \narea” of an individual health faci",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Discusses the difficulty of obtaining reliable population denominators, especially for facility catchment areas, and recommends analyses that avoid them, such as trends in absolute numbers and facility-based denominators. It also notes other information sources that help interpret district data.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-369381/blocks/prose-sec-008",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Principles of this guidance",
      "pageStart": 16,
      "pageEnd": 16,
      "target": "../sections/sec-008-14-principles-of-this-guidance.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "This guidance is based on concepts and indicators presented in the Toolkit documents General principles and \nIntegrated health services analysis: national level. \nThese documents include detailed discussions on data analysis concepts and individual indicators. The focus of \nthe district and facility level guidance is on practical data analysis needs at these operational levels of the health \nsystem. The data analysis approach of this guidance is based on five principles, listed in Box 1. \nBox 1: Principles of this guidance",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Introduces the five principles behind the guidance, building on the toolkit's general principles and national-level documents.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-369381/blocks/prose-sec-009",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
