    {
      "id": "library/10665-369381/blocks/prose-sec-010",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Focused analysis - using a limited set of indicators",
      "pageStart": 16,
      "pageEnd": 17,
      "target": "../sections/sec-010-142-focused-analysis---using-a-limited-set-of-in.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "3. Standardization – of indicators, analyses and visualizations \n4. Data quality assessment – along with analysis\n5. Purpose-oriented analysis – for management and planning\n1.4.1 Integration – across programmes and services \nTo make informed decisions, district managers need data that reflect performance across a wide range of domains \nand programmes: from coverage of immunization to utilization of financial resources. In this guidance, integrated \nanalysis refers to the presentation of indicators from these multiple domains and programmes in ways that they \ncan be reviewed together easily. Th",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains the principles of integration across programmes and of focusing on a limited set of tracer indicators to flag problems. It also discusses keeping disaggregation manageable to protect data quality, noting that WHO does not recommend sex disaggregation of routine childhood immunization data.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-369381/blocks/prose-sec-011",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Standardization - of indicators, analyses and visualizations",
      "pageStart": 17,
      "pageEnd": 18,
      "target": "../sections/sec-011-143-standardization---of-indicators-analyses-and.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Standardization of data elements and indicators enables comparison over time and among places, populations and \nprogrammes. The way the indicators are visualized can also be standardized: a set of standard charts, tables, etc. can be \ndefined and grouped in a standard dashboard. \nThis document presents a sample set of standard dashboards for integrated health services analysis. There are \ndashboards for two analysis levels (health facility and district) and two timeframes (monthly and annual). Section 1.5 \ndiscusses ways of visualizing data with charts and tables. Section 1.6 introduces the sa",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes standardizing indicators, analyses and visualizations so data can be compared, and introduces sample dashboards for facility and district levels over monthly and annual timeframes. It warns against cluttered electronic dashboards and stresses assessing and acknowledging data quality alongside analysis.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-369381/blocks/prose-sec-012",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Purpose-oriented analysis - for management and planning",
      "pageStart": 18,
      "pageEnd": 19,
      "target": "../sections/sec-012-145-purpose-oriented-analysis---for-management-a.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The purpose of RHIS data is to facilitate management decision-making. This guidance offers recommendations for \nanalysis of RHIS data to support decisions taken at district and facility levels. Analysis of data from recent months \nshould inform short-term decisions, while review of year-to-year trends helps to guide longer-term planning. \nTable 1.1 provides a framework summarizing these types of analyses and presents some examples of management \nactions informed by each type of analysis.\nNote that some of the long-term analyses relate to health resources; these analyses, while drawing upon RHI",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains that routine data should inform short-term decisions from recent months and longer-term planning from yearly trends, with a framework table linking analysis types to management actions such as redeploying resources or adjusting supervision.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-369381/blocks/prose-sec-013",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Analysis, interpretation and communication of RHIS findings",
      "pageStart": 19,
      "pageEnd": 19,
      "target": "../sections/sec-013-15-analysis-interpretation-and-communication-of.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "empty"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-369381/blocks/prose-sec-014",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Ways to analyse RHIS data",
      "pageStart": 19,
      "pageEnd": 25,
      "target": "../sections/sec-014-151-ways-to-analyse-rhis-data.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Raw data are rarely suitable for decision-making. They cannot be interpreted easily and may even be misleading. \nFirst, the data should be “cleaned” – reviewed for completeness and consistency and, where possible, corrected. \nThe data can then be visualized and analysed using charts and tables to enable easy identification and interpretation \nof key findings. \nMost analyses involve some form of comparison:  comparison over time (e.g. trends in malaria deaths); comparison \nbetween diseases (e.g. cases of malaria versus cases of pneumonia); comparison among health facilities (e.g. \nantenatal cli",
